hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today we're going to be  talking about cytopathic effects so  first what are cytopathic effects well  they are observable cell abnormalities  so basically ways in which the cells  become abnormal that we can observe  sometimes you need like a high-powered  microscope and special stains to observe  certain aside empathic effects sometimes  you can see them on a low powered  microscope it just depends on what kind  of cytopathic effect you're looking for  and what kind of cell type but so these  are actually caused by viral infections  and sometimes psychopathic effects are  called cpgs so let's talk about a few  different types now different viruses  infecting different cell types will  cause different kinds of cytopathic  effects but some of the ones that  doctors will be looking for is a change  in cellular shape especially cells it  should be flat becoming more rounded  also a loss of surface adherence so this  is when cells usually like they'd like  to grow attached to some kind of  substrate some kind of surface and if  they've been infected by a virus that  has basically mess these cells up  they'll stop adhering to a surface and  just start sort of floating in in the  medium  there's also shrinking of the nucleus so  the nucleus getting smaller another one  that they will look for is inclusion  bodies  so you may might never heard of this  before but inclusion bodies are  basically just aggregates of protein  that can collect in the nucleus or in  the cytoplasm basically wherever the  virus is dividing we're so the site of  viral replication you'll see these  aggregates of protein and that's what we  call inclusion bodies  another type of cpe is the formation of  multinucleated since the shell so since  this ax are when you have multiple cells  fuse together so you have one large sort  of overly large structure there's a lot  of different nuclei in it that came from  a lot of individual cells that are fused  together another possible CDE is  disruption of host cell chromatin so  when the host cell's DNA becomes  disrupted and that sort of disorganized  that's a type of CPE another one is cell  lysis remember that lysis simply needs  to first open a pop to tissue the first  open averse to part so cell lysis is  when a cell breaks open and this is cell  death that the cell dies might undergo  slices another type of CPE probably the  worst kind is transformation of the host  cell into an immortal cell immortal  cells are another name for cancer so  basically this is characterized by  something called loss of senescence  senescence senescence is the ability to  stop dividing and we obviously want  ourselves to  divide uncontrollably but when and  that's called senescence but when cells  lose senescence they can no longer stop  dividing they just start dividing  dividing dividing out of control  we call them immortal cells and this is  what leads to cancer as tumors now let's  talk about some examples of doctors  using psychopathic effects as a  diagnostic tool a really common one that  you've probably heard of is a pap smear  so once young women reach a certain age  it's recommended that they get a pap  smear every year and this is where a  doctor collects a sample from from the  woman's cervix so to get cervical cells  and those cervical cells are actually  viewed under a microscope and assessed  for different kinds of cees and they're  specifically looking for the CPEs that  are caused by HPV infection human  papilloma virus especially those that  would be indicative of cancer because  HPV is certainly well known for causing  cervical cancer so pap smears are  looking for these CPEs in order to  diagnose fat another way that CPAs can  be used diagnostically is with a  hepatitis C infection I have behind us C  infections and often be diagnosed based  on something called liver steatosis  this is basically the abnormal retention  of lipids in a cell so that's one of  these observable cell abnormalities that  can be linked back to a hepatitis C  infection if you're interested in  learning more about viruses check out my  video on introduction to viruses where  you can learn about viral replication  viral genomes different kinds of shapes  and structures and that is all for today  so thanks for watching and I'll see you  next time
 

